        <owl:annotatedTarget>Follicular dendritic cells (FDCs) are specialized cells found in B cell follicles in secondary lymphoid organs such as lymph nodes and spleen. FDCs play a key role in the immune response by presenting antigens to B-cells, which are responsible for producing antibodies. They accomplish this by acquiring antigens via multiple pathways and retaining them in non-degradative endosomal vesicles which cycle to the cell surface, allowing B-cells to encounter and recognize native antigens. In addition, FDCs secrete various cytokines and chemokines that direct immune cells to sites of infection or inflammation.
FDCs are unique among immune cells in that they do not originate from hematopoietic stem cells, but rather from stromal cells in the lymphoid tissues. They are characterized by their distinctive shape, with long, branching processes that form a network within the follicle. FDCs are also distinguished by the presence of complement receptors, Fc receptors, and other molecules that enable them to capture and present antigens to B-cells.
