    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000861"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.org/dc/terms/description"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The elicited macrophage, also commonly referred to as an activated macrophage, is a specialized type of cell that forms a critical part of the immune response. These cells originate from monocytes, a type of white blood cell that circulates in the bloodstream. In response to infection, inflammation, or tissue injury, monocytes leave the bloodstream, enters the inflamed tissue, and differentiates into a macrophage. The term &quot;elicited&quot; indicates that these cells have been activated or stimulated, usually through exposure to mediators such as lipopolysaccharide (LPS) or interferon-gamma (IFN-gamma), and have acquired enhanced microbicidal and tumoricidal capabilities.
